From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Smith <scott@newtrostudios.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 18:40:02 -0400
Subject: remotesmith(0306): changing the permanent password ends sessions that
 used it

AC-39. Upstream leaves every session up when the host's password
changes, so a controller that logged in with the old password keeps
control. Each change of the stored permanent password (through the IPC
setter, or a config sync applied by the other process) now bumps an
epoch. Every authenticated connection records one: a permanent-password
login the epoch taken before the stored password was read for it (so a
change racing the validation cannot land the old password on the new
epoch), any other login the epoch at authorization. A login whose epoch
is already stale at validation or authorization is refused, and a
connection's once-a-second timer closes it with a no-retry reason once
the epoch moved on (port-forward tunnels on their 30 s timer). The host's
30 s resume cache (recent sessions) records each entry's epoch: entries
from before a change are void, and a resumed login carries the original
login's epoch. The
no-retry close also drops its 30 s reconnect session, and the
controller's reconnect policy (0302) never retries that reason, so the
saved credential has to be updated.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
